living alone because they don't have a choice. I also think, actually I know from personal experience that one big myth we have about loneliness is that it's something that happens to us and we have no control over it. I think that's also incorrect. We have a lot more control over our experience of loneliness whether we fear it or run towards it than we think. And people who live alone often have a better relationship with that feeling because they understand, firstly, that it's something you have to get comfortable with, that sometimes it's unavoidable, and secondly, that loneliness is nothing more than a signal to reach out and connect. The same way hunger tells us to eat or thirst tells us to drink. People who live alone know it's true meaning. They know that it's just a messenger, and therefore

56
00:35:46.000 --> 00:36:20.000
they initiate more protective actions like making weekly plans, joining a social, sport team compared to those who may have inbuilt company. It doesn't mean it doesn't get lonely at times. The first night I spent alone in my new place, I was super anxious and felt super odd and super strange, but everything is a trade-off. Sometimes you have to lose something to gain something better and I think that applies to everything in life, not just our living environment. You might lose convenience and a certain baseline level of social interaction.

57
00:36:20.000 --> 00:37:05.000
and run-ins that you're used to, but you also gain a lot of freedom and independence. It's about recognizing which of those things is more important to you, which of them you value. It also means that you're going to have people over whenever you want for as long as you want. They can have a sleep over. No one, you don't need to get anyone's permission. It actually might even make seeing your friends easier because you can do it in the environment that you want to do it in without having to ask other people. I also think that in that solitude, even when you don't have people around, you get to me more authentic, more responsible, more conscientious, perhaps even more feeling, more in touch with your emotions because there are less distractions that you can go and seek out.

